  "notes": "⛔ **\"Merging East Coast drums with West Coast sounds\" is a stated goal, and it is authored as a contradiction the model holds on purpose**: the drums are the most syncopated and least quantized of the producer group (`syncopation` 0.62, `quantizeStrength` 0.77) because he programmed his own patterns to emulate live drummers rather than using loops, while everything above them stays West Coast. ⚠ **The pitch-shift coupling on an SP-1200 is the entry's sharpest single fact and it is authored as note length, not tone**: \"higher = faster and shorter\", so the bass is short and percussive rather than sustained — `boom_chick` rather than the genre's `independent_riff`, `glideProb` 0.06 (the lowest here) and no legato behaviour asked for anywhere. Harmony arrives as sampled Moog and sampled jazz instrumentation, so voicings are monophonic or two-note and \"progressions reduce to `i–VII` or a static `i`\" — that is quoted, so `impliedByRiff` is set, `maxChords` is 2 and `chordDurationBeats` plus three harmonic rhythms carry the chord part's variety rather than families he does not play. `high` is the research's own rating, and it is the only entry here whose harmonic claim is a direct quotation rather than a genre inference. ⚠ Not modelled: triggering samples from the drum pads rather than over MIDI is a workflow, not a parameter, and the 12-bit grit is a tone. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 47 to 43 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 44 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 4.",
